# libFuzzer writes coverage-increasing corpus units (40-hex-char SHA1 names) and
# crash/leak/timeout artifacts when an exploratory run is pointed at a corpus
# dir. Only the human-named seed files (csi_up, fg, ascii, …) are tracked — keep
# discovered units and crash artifacts out of git. Stage seeds explicitly; never
# `git add corpus/<target>` wholesale.
crash-*
leak-*
timeout-*
oom-*
# 40-hex-char libFuzzer corpus unit names (SHA1 of the input):
corpus/*/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]
